What is the Two Pointers technique?
Two Pointers is an algorithmic technique that uses two indices to traverse an array, string, or linked list efficiently. 
It reduces unnecessary comparisons and often improves the time complexity from O(n²) to O(n) while using O(1) extra space.

When do we use Two Pointers?
Use it when:
Sorted arrays
Pair or triplet sum problems
Palindrome checking
Remove duplicates
Sliding window problems
Linked list traversal

3. Types of Two Pointers

1. Opposite Direction
One pointer starts at the beginning and the other at the end.
Used for sorted arrays, pair sum, palindrome, 3Sum.

2. Fast & Slow Pointers
Both pointers move in the same direction, but one moves faster.
Used for remove duplicates, move zeros, linked list cycle, middle node.

3. Sliding Window
Two pointers create a variable-size window.
Used for longest substring, minimum/maximum subarray.

4. Fixed Window
Two pointers maintain a fixed-size window.
Used for maximum sum or average of K elements.

5. Partitioning (Dutch National Flag)
Multiple pointers divide the array into sections.
Used for Sort Colors and Quick Sort partitioning.

4. Time Complexity
Time: O(n)
Space: O(1)
